[97m[49m⏺[39m 1. Terminals are text-based interfaces for interacting with computers.
  2. The first terminals were electromechanical teletypes connected to mainframes.
  3. Unix terminals follow the POSIX standard for compatibility.                                                                                                
  4. Bash is the most commonly used shell in modern terminals.                    
  5. Terminal emulators like iTerm2 and GNOME Terminal provide graphical interfaces for shell access.                                                           
  6. The ctrl+c keyboard shortcut sends a SIGINT signal to terminate running processes.              
  7. Most terminals support ANSI color codes for colorized output.                                   
  8. The [94mls[39m command lists files and directories in the current directory.              
  9. Tab completion automatically finishes commands and filenames when you press the Tab key.        
  10. Terminal multiplexers like tmux allow multiple sessions within a single window.        
  11. The [94mcd[39m command changes the current working directory.                                          
  12. Environment variables in terminals are set with the [94mexport[39m command.                            
  13. The shell prompt typically shows the current user and directory.                               
  14. Pipes ([94m|[39m) allow output from one command to be input to another.                                
  15. Redirection operators ([94m>[39m, [94m<[39m, [94m>>[39m) control where data flows in terminals.                
  16. Command history can be accessed using the up arrow key in most terminals.              
  17. The [94mgrep[39m command searches for text patterns in files.                                  
  18. Regular expressions enable powerful pattern matching in terminal commands.             
  19. The [94mfind[39m command locates files based on various criteria.                      
  20. SSH allows secure remote access to terminals on other computers.          
  21. The [94mchmod[39m command changes file and directory permissions.              
  22. The [94msudo[39m command executes commands with superuser privileges.             
  23. Package managers like apt, brew, and pacman install software via terminals.
  24. The [94mman[39m command displays manual pages for terminal commands.               
  25. Aliases allow users to create shortcuts for frequently used commands.      
